    {
      "id": "v1/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top1/r01/G1",
      "evidence_sha256": "aa1c678e5ecf098c37d2f27ff4fa6a4cbb1984ba0da679f61bacaa92f9fce6d7",
      "assessment": "R1 G1 correctly identifies the loss of 0.01025 relative to R0, the 20 percent rise in interval-0 inside displacement, and concentrated Q2/Q3 losses. H1 [7,5,5,5] extends the dose ladder; H2 [6,6,5,5] moves the extra tick to interval 1. Its claim that 25 mid-window steps necessarily give more cumulative displacement than 16 early steps is unsupported by the measured per-step displacement and integration weights.",
      "decision_update": "Require an integrated effective-displacement comparison, or label cumulative-control ranking unknown; do not substitute number of updates for integrated movement. A second small dose step can discriminate under-control from proxy mismatch, but specify a comparable-parent response and retain the adverse Mean evidence rather than describing it simply as no movement.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "The intervention measurably changed geometry while affinity did not broadly improve; this is a counterexample to the inference that proportional displacement predicts proportional affinity. New proposals restart from a selected mixed parent population, so their later aggregate gain cannot establish the R1 window's causal effect.",
