    {
      "id": "v1/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top1/r01/G3",
      "evidence_sha256": "42b6c5a7835b4afcd001d90d1313c7790b245bde6e34bcfbd360bb538ffefc9d",
      "assessment": "R1 G3's tool-request rationale initially reverses incumbent-minus-latest +0.00753 into a gain. Its final diagnosis corrects the sign and locates the actual approximately -0.7204 loss in Q2. H1 [5,5,6,7] intensifies both late windows; H2 [6,5,5,5] reverts the terminal edit while increasing the first window. Several forecasts still call the prior change +0.008, and the stated whole-range inertness conclusion exceeds the local evidence.",
      "decision_update": "Reconcile sign corrections in every downstream hypothesis and public memory, not only in the diagnosis. Require separate physical-control, score-response and identifiability conclusions. A relocation involving two intervals must remain a joint test; a local null cannot falsify all allowed amplitudes or prove a universal early-window advantage.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "The observed large negative candidate change is itself evidence against the claim that sub-milliangstrom guidance cannot affect affinity. Shared random numbers permit discrete branches to diverge when model probabilities change; absent independent repeats, calling these changes sampling noise is an unverified explanation.",
